For a text node’s character offsets, Ladybird now reads each character’s
x from the glyph runs layout shaped, and splits a cluster’s advance
evenly among its code units. Blink also reads layout’s shaping:
AXBlockFlowIterator::GetCharacterLayoutPixelOffsets() takes the
layout fragment’s TextShapeResult() and reads per-character advances
with ShapeResult::IndividualCharacterRanges(). Gecko’s nsTextFrame::
GetCharacterRectsInRange() also reads the frame’s mTextRun, with
cluster boundaries and GetAdvanceWidth(), but gives every character
of a cluster the whole cluster’s rect, rather than an even share.
WebKit’s AccessibilityRenderObject::textRuns() measures each whole
TextRun again, into a GlyphBuffer through ComplexTextController or
WidthIterator, so it shapes runs rather than single code points, but
doesn’t reuse layout’s shaping.
